WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.745 --> 00:00:10.266
Comparison is where course thinking gets real. This page holds two or three sources next to each other so you can see what each one argues.

2
00:00:10.266 --> 00:00:18.930
Pick two or three sources. Use the picker on the right. Choose sources that speak to the same question from different angles.

3
00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:26.222
Read the side-by-side cards. Each card keeps the source's core idea, evidence type, and limits visible.

4
00:00:26.222 --> 00:00:37.226
Work the guided comparison. Pick a lens, then write what the sources share, how they differ, and why the differences matter. Open the worked example if you want a model first.

5
00:00:37.226 --> 00:00:48.243
Press Synthesize when you are ready. The synthesis builds a frame across your chosen sources. Use it as a thinking scaffold, not as sentences to copy, and reveal the model comparison only after you have written your own.

6
00:00:48.243 --> 00:00:56.833
Save your comparison. Save my comparison keeps your notes, and the synthesis can be copied, printed, or saved to your notes.

7
00:00:56.833 --> 00:01:02.082
Comparisons are never graded. They exist to sharpen your written work.

8
00:01:02.082 --> 00:01:09.123
Where next: Carry your comparison insight into this week's reflection or your Personal Cartography.
